WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.640
Turn your head,

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.310
and the chair leaves your view.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:03.800
But not your mind.

00:00:04.187 --> 00:00:05.537
You still know where it is,

00:00:05.557 --> 00:00:06.962
what one step would reveal,

00:00:06.982 --> 00:00:08.547
and how to get back to it.

00:00:08.717 --> 00:00:10.327
That's a spatial state.

00:00:10.703 --> 00:00:12.558
Visual foundation models learn

00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:15.133
from huge amounts of images and video.

00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:17.193
But do they hold a spatial state?

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:23.752
We put ten frozen models to the test:

00:00:23.772 --> 00:00:26.653
image and video encoders, a 3D model,

00:00:26.673 --> 00:00:28.815
video generators, a world model,

00:00:28.835 --> 00:00:30.890
and a multimodal LLM.

00:00:31.253 --> 00:00:32.968
Each model watches a camera move

00:00:32.988 --> 00:00:34.331
through indoor scenes,

00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:37.153
frame by frame, never seeing the future.

00:00:37.323 --> 00:00:38.693
A small trained probe

00:00:38.713 --> 00:00:40.743
then reads out what its features know.

00:00:41.103 --> 00:00:42.773
We ask four questions.

00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:44.460
Can it read the visible scene:

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.473
depth, pose, and objects?

00:00:46.643 --> 00:00:47.940
Does it remember objects

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.453
after they leave the view?

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.280
Can it predict

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.043
what it will see after a move?

00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:53.288
And can it pick a move

00:00:53.308 --> 00:00:55.183
that brings it closer to a goal?

00:00:56.475 --> 00:00:57.940
Here's what we found.

00:00:58.120 --> 00:00:59.980
No model wins everywhere.

00:01:00.337 --> 00:01:02.505
Fast3R reads camera pose

00:01:02.525 --> 00:01:04.967
nine times better than DINOv2,

00:01:04.987 --> 00:01:06.292
and best locates objects

00:01:06.312 --> 00:01:07.567
that left the view.

00:01:07.737 --> 00:01:09.592
VideoMAE is second,

00:01:09.612 --> 00:01:12.187
despite weaker readout of the visible scene.

00:01:12.578 --> 00:01:13.595
Seeing a scene well

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:15.273
does not mean remembering it.

00:01:15.443 --> 00:01:16.488
Across models,

00:01:16.508 --> 00:01:18.943
remembering tracks camera pose instead.

00:01:19.303 --> 00:01:20.578
And for future prediction,

00:01:20.598 --> 00:01:23.493
DINOv2 and V-JEPA 2 take the lead.

00:01:24.763 --> 00:01:27.083
Where and when you read also matters.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:28.453
In V-JEPA 2,

00:01:28.473 --> 00:01:30.253
prediction peaks at the last layer

00:01:30.273 --> 00:01:31.883
and persistence earlier.

00:01:32.053 --> 00:01:34.953
In VideoMAE, it's the other way around.

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.870
And more history is not always better.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.975
Going from 8 frames to 24

00:01:39.995 --> 00:01:42.300
hurts four in five comparisons,

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.075
and makes object persistence worse

00:01:44.095 --> 00:01:45.900
for every model we tested.

00:01:47.158 --> 00:01:49.973
Next, decodable is not the same as used.

00:01:50.337 --> 00:01:53.087
For V-JEPA 2, simply copying the last feature

00:01:53.107 --> 00:01:55.377
beats a learned one-step predictor.

00:01:55.547 --> 00:01:57.487
And VideoMAE,

00:01:57.507 --> 00:01:59.750
the most accurate localizer in this test,

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.412
shows no reliable benefit

00:02:01.432 --> 00:02:02.987
from its visual history.

00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:06.128
V-JEPA 2 can also predict

00:02:06.148 --> 00:02:07.853
future features natively.

00:02:08.023 --> 00:02:09.193
But hand those predictions

00:02:09.213 --> 00:02:11.893
to a depth reader trained on real features,

00:02:11.913 --> 00:02:16.155
and the error jumps from 0.18 to 0.25,

00:02:16.175 --> 00:02:17.568
worse than a layout prior

00:02:17.588 --> 00:02:19.203
that ignores the input.

00:02:19.570 --> 00:02:20.620
Retrain the reader,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.170
and the error falls to 0.22.

00:02:23.355 --> 00:02:24.885
Some geometry survives,

00:02:24.905 --> 00:02:26.800
but the interface has changed.

00:02:28.085 --> 00:02:30.090
Finally, acting toward a goal.

00:02:30.453 --> 00:02:33.008
Picking the better of two candidate moves works,

00:02:33.028 --> 00:02:34.963
and clearly depends on the goal.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:35.878
But what if the probe

00:02:35.898 --> 00:02:37.663
must output the move itself?

00:02:38.055 --> 00:02:40.135
Swap in a goal from another scene,

00:02:40.155 --> 00:02:41.785
and the predicted direction swings

00:02:41.805 --> 00:02:44.090
by 30 to 46 degrees.

00:02:44.260 --> 00:02:45.360
Yet the correct goal

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:47.820
does not reliably beat a shuffled one.

00:02:48.187 --> 00:02:49.172
The needle turns,

00:02:49.192 --> 00:02:50.767
but it doesn't point the way.

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.727
Goal sensitivity is not goal utility.

00:02:54.987 --> 00:02:56.817
Two chains link the levels:

00:02:56.837 --> 00:02:59.317
memory and action go with camera pose,

00:02:59.337 --> 00:03:01.997
while prediction goes with the scene's content.

00:03:02.353 --> 00:03:03.773
To judge whether a vision model

00:03:03.793 --> 00:03:05.273
holds a spatial state,

00:03:05.293 --> 00:03:07.473
don't just ask what can be decoded.

00:03:07.653 --> 00:03:09.758
Ask whether it can be kept beyond view,

00:03:09.778 --> 00:03:12.383
updated under motion, and used to act.
